DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  _n_e_t_g_r_o_u_p  is  a SunOS invention.  A netgroup database is a list of
       string triples  (_h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e,  _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e,  _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e)  or  other  netgroup
       names.   Any of the elements in a triple can be empty, which means that
       anything matches.  The functions described here  allow  access  to  the
       netgroup  databases.  The file _/_e_t_c_/_n_s_s_w_i_t_c_h_._c_o_n_f defines what database
       is searched.

       The sseettnneettggrreenntt() call defines the netgroup that will  be  searched  by
       subsequent  ggeettnneettggrreenntt()  calls.  The ggeettnneettggrreenntt() function retrieves
       the next netgroup entry, and returns pointers in _h_o_s_t, _u_s_e_r, _d_o_m_a_i_n.  A
       null pointer means that the corresponding  entry  matches  any  string.
       The  pointers  are valid only as long as there is no call to other net‐
       group‐related functions.  To avoid this problem you  can  use  the  GNU
       function  ggeettnneettggrreenntt__rr()  that  stores  the  strings  in  the supplied
       buffer.  To free all allocated buffers use eennddnneettggrreenntt().

       In most cases you want to check only if the  triplet  (_h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e,  _u_s_e_r_‐
       _n_a_m_e,  _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e)  is  a member of a netgroup.  The function iinnnneettggrr()
       can be used for this without calling the above three functions.  Again,
       a null pointer is a wildcard and matches any string.  The  function  is
       thread‐safe.

